{"chapter": "sheaves", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "006P", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$\\mathcal{O}_1 \\to \\mathcal{O}_2$", "MATH_0002": "$X$", "MATH_0003": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "MATH_0004": "$\\mathcal{O}_2$", "MATH_0005": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "MATH_0006": "$\\mathcal{O}_1$", "MATH_0007": "$$\n\\mathcal{O}_1 \\times \\mathcal{F}\n\\to\n\\mathcal{O}_2 \\times \\mathcal{F}\n\\to\n\\mathcal{F}.\n$$", "MATH_0008": "$\\mathcal{F}_{\\mathcal{O}_1}$", "MATH_0009": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "MATH_0010": "$$\n\\textit{PMod}(\\mathcal{O}_2)\n\\longrightarrow\n\\textit{PMod}(\\mathcal{O}_1)\n$$", "MATH_0011": "$\\mathcal{O}_1$", "MATH_0012": "$\\mathcal{G}$", "MATH_0013": "$\\mathcal{O}_2$", "MATH_0014": "$\\mathcal{O}_2 \\otimes_{p, \\mathcal{O}_1} \\mathcal{G}$", "MATH_0015": "$$\n\\left(\\mathcal{O}_2 \\otimes_{p, \\mathcal{O}_1} \\mathcal{G}\\right)(U)\n=\n\\mathcal{O}_2(U) \\otimes_{\\mathcal{O}_1(U)} \\mathcal{G}(U)\n$$", "MATH_0016": "$p$", "MATH_0017": "$$\n\\textit{PMod}(\\mathcal{O}_1)\n\\longrightarrow\n\\textit{PMod}(\\mathcal{O}_2)\n$$", "STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent", "STRUCT_0002": "\\medskip", "STRUCT_0003": "\\noindent", "TEXTITCLOSE_0001": "}", "TEXTITCLOSE_0002": "}", "TEXTITCLOSE_0003": "}", "TEXTITOPEN_0001": "{\\it ", "TEXTITOPEN_0002": "{\\it ", "TEXTITOPEN_0003": "{\\it "}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:a7f9bd362c5a0a85ec1591ab725d0ea776a5822ae21a726e1600c238eed1d3ac", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:98657a952ea0f39a3b2b314944626c5e948f9dd24605b79d5702170694cea903", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nSuppose that <MATH_0001> is a\nmorphism of presheaves of rings on <MATH_0002>. In this case,\nif <MATH_0003> is a presheaf of <MATH_0004>-modules\nthen we can think of <MATH_0005> as a presheaf of\n<MATH_0006>-modules by using the composition\n<MATH_0007>\nWe sometimes denote this by <MATH_0008>\nto indicate the restriction of rings. We call this\nthe <TEXTITOPEN_0001>restriction of <MATH_0009><TEXTITCLOSE_0001>. We obtain the\nrestriction functor\n<MATH_0010>\n\n<STRUCT_0002><STRUCT_0003>\nOn the other hand, given a presheaf of <MATH_0011>-modules\n<MATH_0012>\nwe can construct a presheaf of <MATH_0013>-modules\n<MATH_0014>\nby the rule\n<MATH_0015>\nThe index <MATH_0016> stands for ``presheaf'' and not ``point''.\nThis presheaf is called the <TEXTITOPEN_0002>tensor product presheaf<TEXTITCLOSE_0002>. We obtain\nthe <TEXTITOPEN_0003>change of rings<TEXTITCLOSE_0003> functor\n<MATH_0017>\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:502e7510b443f4e40a424877e049f3c7b55cc2153364a8034dd94112a896ab2b", "unit_id": "tag:006P:prose-0002"}
{"chapter": "sheaves", "node_kind": "lemma", "parent_tag": "006P", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$X$", "MATH_0002": "$\\mathcal{O}_1$", "MATH_0003": "$\\mathcal{O}_2$", "MATH_0004": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "MATH_0005": "$\\mathcal{G}$", "MATH_0006": "$$\n\\Hom_{\\mathcal{O}_1}(\\mathcal{G}, \\mathcal{F}_{\\mathcal{O}_1})\n=\n\\Hom_{\\mathcal{O}_2}(\n\\mathcal{O}_2 \\otimes_{p, \\mathcal{O}_1} \\mathcal{G},\n\\mathcal{F}\n)\n$$"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{lemma}\n\\label{lemma-adjointness-tensor-restrict-presheaves}\n", "suffix": "\\end{lemma}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:131e639dfc07fb2fcf185a5db3e83f45efa704869960b19b6c5fb4386c7d179b", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:1ac7fe263be4c08ae556a22182aaf0c03b8e219ddcec80e6c68653ae8ee9718c", "source_text": "With <MATH_0001>, <MATH_0002>, <MATH_0003>, <MATH_0004> and\n<MATH_0005> as above there exists a canonical bijection\n<MATH_0006>\nIn other words, the restriction and change of rings functors\nare adjoint to each other.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:1ba010ee10b6f2054fce0ee920fb9972a7535f2236818c8cc0703bb0086c7803", "unit_id": "tag:006R:statement"}
{"chapter": "sheaves", "node_kind": "proof", "parent_tag": "006P", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$A \\to B$"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{proof}\n", "suffix": "\\end{proof}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:98e96aab4a499c5ef44a83e1b263bd055c8643aea9540d70a4eab383cbc79da2", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:d67e7e344d46507124f3b02ae57ddae7eac639714e75580a536bd96529e58983", "source_text": "This follows from the fact that for a ring map\n<MATH_0001> the restriction functor and the change\nof ring functor are adjoint to each other.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:1e9d262c6cafcd972077b3a72fc874f4b88753eff621f8a663b8e72ff707beda", "unit_id": "tag:006R:proof"}
